---
/**
 * The front page: what to do, and how much of it can be demonstrated.
 *
 * The argument is one contrast, made under one rule. Fees, taxes and account placement
 * settle against your own old portfolio in about three and a half months; the whole construction
 * against a cheap index fund takes about thirty-one years. Both are `T = (z·s/e)²` at
 * 90% confidence, and holding them to one rule is the point — the detection question,
 * which asks when a *study* could separate an effect from zero, lives on `/how-sure/`
 * and is labelled as such there.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Rung from "~/components/Rung.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Do the certain things first"
  bareTitle
  description="Fees, accounts and where you hold things are worth about 1.09% a year against the portfolio you already own. Beating a cheap index fund is a different question, and we cannot demonstrate it."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>The answer</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Do the certain things first</h1>

    <MarginNote label="What this page is">
      <p>
        An answer to one question: what should I actually do with my money, and how much of it can be
        demonstrated? Checked against <code>docs/research/</code> on <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Cut your fees. Use the tax-advantaged accounts you already have. Put each fund in whichever account taxes
    it least.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Where this comes from">
      <p>
        Fund filings and tax statute, not a backtest. The largest single line is the capital gain an active mutual
        fund hands you in December and an exchange-traded fund does not. It read 0.89% before the ledger was
        revised.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Against the portfolio a normal person would otherwise be holding, those three moves save about
    <Figure id="structural-budget" inline />. They are close to certain, because they come out of contracts, tax
    statutes and arithmetic rather than out of a forecast about markets. And you would find out whether you got
    them right in roughly <Figure id="structural-budget-horizon" inline />, which is the shortest honest answer
    anywhere on this site.</p>

    <p>Do those first. Then read the rest of this page, which is mostly about how much less we can prove than you
    would like.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout label="The three yardsticks, never added" class="mt-10">
    <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Figure id="structural-budget" />
      <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" />
      <Figure id="behaviour-gap" />
    </div>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Better than what</Rail>

    <h2>Better than what, exactly</h2>

    <MarginNote>
      <p>
        A saving measured against your own current holdings says nothing about whether you beat an index fund.
        The two never add, and the research code raises rather than summing them.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>That figure is measured against the portfolio you would otherwise own. It is not measured against an index,
    and the difference decides whether the number applies to you at all.</p>

    <p>Say your money sits in an employer plan full of actively managed funds charging
    <Figure id="active-fund-fee" inline />, plus a taxable account that hands you a capital-gains distribution
    every December you never asked for, plus an IRA quietly holding an international fund whose foreign
    withholding you can never claim back. Then most of that 1.09% is genuinely sitting there. Go and take it.</p>

    <p>Now say you already own one cheap total-market index fund, in one account, and you never touch it.</p>

    <p>Your number is close to zero. We have nothing to sell you.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Read as a bound">
      <p>
        An outer bound rather than a distribution. The bottom assumes a sheltered account, an index-fund starting
        point and a static investor already paying 0.40%. The top assumes every condition holds at once, and the
        conditions are correlated.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Most people sit between those two, which is why the honest outer bound on the package runs
    <Figure id="structural-budget-range" inline />. That spread is wide because the bottom of it assumes every
    condition fails at once and the top assumes they all hold at once, and the conditions are not independent of
    each other.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="do-this">
    <p>
      Open your plan statement and read one number: the yearly fee on the fund holding most of your money. Under
      0.10% a year, skip to the next section. Over 0.50%, you have just found the largest reliable gain available
      to you today.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The hard part</Rail>

    <h2>Beating a cheap index fund is a different question, and the answer is no</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that costs us something to write.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Source">
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/expected-edge-decomposition.md"
          >Where outperformance can come from</a
        >, as of <span data-numeric>2026-08-17</span>. The drift is assumed rather than measured: the components
        are combined as though independent, and inside one portfolio they are not, so the real dispersion is
        wider.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Add up everything this project has measured that might plausibly beat a plain cheap index fund, keep only
    what survives its own error bars, and you are left with a defensible advantage of about
    <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" inline />. The drift around that is
    <Figure id="index-tracking-error" inline />. In a normal year you would finish somewhere within roughly three
    percentage points either side of the index, for no reason except that you own different things.</p>

    <p>About sixty times as much noise as signal. Hold it for thirty years and your chance of ending ahead of the
    index is <Figure id="odds-ahead-of-cheap-index" inline />.</p>

    <p>This is a coin flip, and saying so is the finding.</p>

    <MarginNote label="One rule, twice">
      <p>
        Both waits are <code>T = (z·s/e)²</code> at 90% confidence, the closed form in
        <a href="https://github.com/FlanaganSe/kelly-portfolios/blob/main/docs/research/expected-edge-decomposition.md"
          >the edge decomposition</a
        >. When a <em>study</em> could separate an effect from zero is a different question with a different
        answer, and <a href="/how-sure/">how sure we are</a> keeps the two apart.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>The arithmetic underneath is not a matter of opinion, and it is worth understanding once. At that much
    drift, the smallest yearly advantage that thirty years of your own account could actually demonstrate at 90%
    confidence is <Figure id="smallest-demonstrable-edge" inline />, roughly fourteen times what we can defend.
    Point the same rule at the whole recommended portfolio, measured against a plain cheap index fund holding the
    same mix, and the wait comes out at <Figure id="time-to-know-portfolio" inline showUnit={false} />.</p>

    <p>Four months for the fees. Thirty-one years for the rest of it, on one rule applied twice, and that contrast
    is the entire argument for doing the boring things first.</p>

    <p>None of this says a cheap index fund cannot be beaten. It says we cannot show you that we can beat one, on
    the data anyone has, and that a site claiming otherwise is either measuring against something easier or has
    not checked how long its own claim would take to prove.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>How sure</Rail>

    <h2>Three yardsticks, three answers, and they never add up</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Why not colour">
      <p>
        A green badge would say two things at once: how sure we are, and whether the news is good. Those are
        different axes, and merging them is the mistake this vocabulary exists to avoid.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Every claim about a portfolio has to finish the sentence "better than what". Three endings make sense, and
    they are three different questions with three unrelated answers.</p>

    <p>Against a cheap index fund, the answer is the coin flip above. Against the portfolio you already hold, the
    answer is the number at the top of this page, and it settles quickly because it carries only
    <Figure id="own-portfolio-tracking-error" inline /> of drift around it. Against what the typical investor
    actually achieves, after buying late and selling into falls, Morningstar's measured ten-year gap is
    <Figure id="behaviour-gap" inline />, and the odds of staying ahead of that for thirty years are
    <Figure id="odds-ahead-of-average-investor" inline />.</p>

    <p>Three answers. Never one total.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[32rem] border-collapse text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">The three benchmarks, their edges, their drift, and how sure we are of each</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">Compared with</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4 text-right">Edge, a year</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4 text-right">Drift, a year</th>
          <th scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2">How sure</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-ink-muted">
        <tr class="border-b border-rule">
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">The portfolio you hold now</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">1.09%</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">0.46%</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="settled" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-rule">
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">A cheap total-market index</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">0.054%</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">3.13%</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="cant-tell" /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">What the average investor actually gets</th>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">0.15%</td>
          <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">1.50%</td>
          <td class="py-2"><Rung level="might" /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <MarginNote label="What it cost us">
      <p>
        Four defects in one line: a truncated range, a rebalancing hurdle avoided booked as a saving, two mutually
        exclusive tactics both counted, and 94.7% of the top figure resting on one holding's undistributed
        accrual. <a href="/how-sure/">The corrections log</a> carries all eight.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Adding them is the most common way a result on this site has turned out to be wrong, and we know because we
    did it. An internal re-derivation found that our own account-placement headline of roughly 0.38% to 0.55% a
    year had been built partly from lines measured against different yardsticks, with a rebalancing cost avoided
    booked as though it were a saving, and two mutually exclusive tactics both counted. The defensible figure
    against a yardstick a real investor could reach is <Figure id="placement-value" inline />. The research code
    now raises an error rather than adding across yardsticks, because a rule nobody can forget to follow is the
    only kind that holds.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="change-your-mind">
    <p>
      A fund shelf that cut its published edge below 0.20% a year, or a decade of real returns in which the funds
      leaning toward cheap and profitable companies trailed the index by more than their stated drift, would move
      the second row of that table from a coin flip to a rejection.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Next</Rail>

    <h2>Where to go from here</h2>

    <p>The ordered checklist is at <a href="/start/">start here</a>, and it is the page to read if you only read
    one. Fees, then accounts, then placement, then your savings rate and your stock-versus-safe split, then
    leaving it alone, and only then anything clever.</p>

    <p><a href="/portfolio/">The portfolio</a> is the concrete version: seven holdings, and which account each one
    belongs in. <a href="/doesnt-work/">What does not work</a> covers market timing, crypto as ballast, crash
    insurance and direct indexing, each rejected on a measurement rather than on principle, each with the number
    that would change our mind. <a href="/stacking/">Stacking</a> explains why owning seven clever things does not
    compound into certainty, and why the way you pay for an add-on matters more than the add-on.
    <a href="/how-sure/">How sure we are</a> sets out the four levels of confidence used everywhere here, and
    lists every error this project caught in itself.</p>

    <p>Start with the fees. That part we can prove.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
